Contract in rk806.h:
```
 *   The board table is validated in full before any regulator is registered
 *   and before any PMIC register is written: an entry that is invalid (empty
 *   name, UNKNOWN source carrying a voltage, limits out of range, unknown
 *   voltage source) is reported and skipped, and the remaining outputs are
 *   still registered.  A regulator_register() failure is handled the same
 *   way: it is reported for that output only.
 *
 *   Initialization is therefore per-output, not all-or-nothing: outputs that
 *   registered stay registered even when the return value reports a failure,
 *   and consumers of a skipped output simply fail to get that supply and keep
 *   the rest.  There is no rollback -- the hardware has already been enabled
 *   for boot_on outputs by this point, so unregistering would only hide the
 *   software view, not undo the power state.
 *
 *   The function is meant to be called once during board bring-up.  It holds
 *   no shadow copy of the registered devices: the regulator framework list is
 *   the only state.
 *
 * Returned Value:
 *   OK (0) if every output was registered; otherwise the first error
 *   encountered (-EINVAL for a rejected board entry, -ENODEV when
 *   regulator_register() refused an output).  The return value summarizes
 *   what happened; it does not mean nothing was registered.
```

